      {
        "section_id": "MA-S2",
        "writer_text": "## LinearRAG overview: Tri‑Graph concept and two‑stage retrieval philosophy\n\nThe LinearRAG system structures retrieval around a Tri‑Graph, a graph whose three node types—entities, sentences, and passages—are linked by sparse adjacency matrices. Retrieval proceeds in two stages: the first stage activates entity nodes to perform local semantic bridging, and the second stage propagates importance across the graph (via personalized PageRank) to select passages for global relevance.",
